WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.093
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.393 --> 00:00:09.849
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.149 --> 00:00:18.467
Unit four: Historical Analysis Texts. Today you will discuss unreal pasts with inverted conditionals such as Had it not been for.

4
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:21.512
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.711
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.039
Primary source.

7
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:33.290
Primary source means an original document or object from the time being studied.

8
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:36.870
A diary is a valuable primary source.

9
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:40.588
Your turn. Say: Primary source.

10
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:44.876
Primary source.

11
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:46.896
Historiography.

12
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:52.124
Historiography means the study of how history has been written and interpreted.

13
00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:55.947
Historiography shows how views of the war changed.

14
00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.911
Your turn. Say: Historiography.

15
00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.279
Historiography.

16
00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:06.656
Historical context.

17
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:12.122
Historical context means the conditions and events surrounding something in the past.

18
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:15.773
Historical context explains why the law was passed.

19
00:01:16.383 --> 00:01:19.860
Your turn. Say: Historical context.

20
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.514
Historical context.

21
00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:26.298
Causation.

22
00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:30.850
Causation means the relationship between a cause and its effect.

23
00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.146
Historians debate the causation of the revolution.

24
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.611
Your turn. Say: Causation.

25
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:41.514
Causation.

26
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:43.228
Bias.

27
00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:47.145
Bias means an unfair leaning towards one side.

28
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.051
The chronicler's bias favoured the king.

29
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.186
Your turn. Say: Bias.

30
00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:56.918
Bias.

31
00:01:57.683 --> 00:01:58.986
Interpretation.

32
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:04.154
Interpretation means a reasoned explanation of what evidence means.

33
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.713
Each interpretation depends on the evidence chosen.

34
00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:10.981
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

35
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.101
Interpretation.

36
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:17.420
Archival research.

37
00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:22.018
Archival research means the study of documents kept in an archive.

38
00:02:22.617 --> 00:02:25.568
Archival research uncovered letters nobody had read.

39
00:02:26.183 --> 00:02:29.614
Your turn. Say: Archival research.

40
00:02:32.467 --> 00:02:34.013
Archival research.

41
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.053
Periodization.

42
00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.907
Periodization means dividing the past into named periods for study.

43
00:02:41.517 --> 00:02:43.992
Periodization can hide gradual change.

44
00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:47.654
Your turn. Say: Periodization.

45
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.640
Periodization.

46
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:55.794
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:02.716
Visal, a historian writes 'Had the harvest not failed, the revolt might never have begun.' What is unusual here?

48
00:03:03.817 --> 00:03:16.224
It is an inverted conditional. Had + subject + past participle replaces If + past perfect. It is formal and common when historians imagine a different past.

49
00:03:17.317 --> 00:03:19.189
What is the result clause?

50
00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:29.672
Usually would / might / could + have + past participle. Had the treaty held, war would have been avoided. It describes an unreal result in the past.

51
00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:32.191
Can we make it negative?

52
00:03:33.283 --> 00:03:44.933
Put not after the subject: Had the king not died. The phrase 'Had it not been for' also works with a noun: Had it not been for the diaries, we would know little.

53
00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.774
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:54.041
Grammar focus: Inverted conditional: Had plus subject plus past participle.

55
00:03:54.341 --> 00:03:55.505
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:56.217 --> 00:03:59.900
Had the harvest not failed, the revolt might never have begun.

57
00:04:00.783 --> 00:04:03.247
Had the treaty held, war would have been avoided.

58
00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:07.427
Had it not been for the diaries, we would know little about the siege.

59
00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:18.417
Had + subject + past participle = If + past perfect. Use would / might / could have + past participle for the unreal result. Add not after the subject for a negative.

60
00:04:19.183 --> 00:04:22.823
Be careful! Don't say: If had we known, we would have acted.

61
00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:27.196
Say: Had we known, we would have acted.

62
00:04:27.496 --> 00:04:29.402
Drop if when you invert with had.

63
00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.400
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:33.934
What If?.

65
00:04:34.234 --> 00:04:38.707
Historians study causation, but they cannot run experiments on the past.

66
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:43.511
Had a primary source been lost, a whole period might have stayed unknown.

67
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:50.041
Archival research often shows that historical context was more complex than later writers claimed.

68
00:04:51.033 --> 00:04:56.353
Had the chronicler been neutral, his bias would not have shaped later historiography so strongly.

69
00:04:57.367 --> 00:05:02.559
Periodization is useful, although dividing the past into periods can hide gradual change.

70
00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:07.529
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.383 --> 00:05:10.905
blank the letter arrived, he would have stayed.

72
00:05:11.205 --> 00:05:14.917
A, Had. B, If had. C, Has.

73
00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:20.025
The answer is A: Had.

74
00:05:21.183 --> 00:05:24.530
An original document from the time studied is a blank source.

75
00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:28.483
A, primary. B, secondary. C, digital.

76
00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:33.870
The answer is A: primary.

77
00:05:35.017 --> 00:05:36.645
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:36.945 --> 00:05:43.338
A, Had we known, we would help.. B, Had we known, we would have helped.. C, Have we known, we would have helped..

79
00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:50.037
The answer is B: Had we known, we would have helped..

80
00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:53.972
An unfair leaning towards one side is blank.

81
00:05:54.272 --> 00:05:58.158
A, periodization. B, causation. C, bias.

82
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.711
The answer is C: bias.

83
00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:08.463
According to the reading, what is useful but may hide gradual change?

84
00:06:08.763 --> 00:06:13.206
A, periodization. B, an archive. C, a diary.

85
00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:19.289
The answer is A: periodization.

86
00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.058
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:28.867
Why might two historians interpret the same primary source differently?

88
00:06:37.683 --> 00:06:39.776
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.076 --> 00:07:09.158
Two historians may read the same primary source differently because they bring different questions, knowledge and assumptions. One may focus on the author's bias, while another stresses the historical context. Had they asked the same question, their conclusions might have matched. Different interpretations are healthy when each is supported by evidence and openly explained, because they push scholars to test their ideas.

90
00:07:09.458 --> 00:07:11.121
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:12.017 --> 00:07:18.373
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and inverted conditional: had plus subject plus past participle.

92
00:07:18.673 --> 00:07:23.670
Today's value is historical awareness. Know the past so you can understand the present.

93
00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.611
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

94
00:07:26.911 --> 00:07:30.225
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

